## ABSTRACT
The development of artificial enzyme mimics has been rapidly growing in recent years, and it is attracting increasing attention owing to their remarkable advantages over natural enzymes. Herein, we developed a general and facile method to fabricate efficient glutathione peroxidase (GPx) mimics by grafting selenium-containing molecules (phenylselenylbromide, PhSeBr) to a $\mathrm{{Zr}(I V)}$ -based $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ framework. In the presence of glutathione (GSH) serving as substrate, the fabricated UiO-66-Se catalysts can catalyze the reduction of hydroperoxides. The as-prepared UiO-66-Se systems show good catalytic activity over three cycles. These high-efficiency ${\mathrm{GPx}}$ mimic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are endowed with excellent thermal and structural stability, providing a promising avenue for the development of artificial enzyme mimics.
## 1 Introduction
Natural enzymes are truly outstanding biocatalysts owing to their high catalytic efficiency as well as chemo, stereo, and regioselectivity under mild conditions [1–4]. However, almost all natural enzymes are proteins with intrinsic drawbacks including instability, high cost, and difficult recycling and regeneration [5, 6]. Recently, the unique catalytic properties of natural enzymes have inspired many scientists to promote the development of artificial enzyme mimic catalysts, which have shown excellent properties such as high stability, easy preparation, robustness under harsh environmental conditions (e.g., methanol, ethanol, and dimethylformamide), long-term storage, and good catalytic selectivity [7, 8]. Various biomimetic catalytic systems have been reviewed by Kirby, Motherwell, Wulff, Huang and others [9–14]. In this context, some important pioneering studies have been carried out on artificial enzymes with peroxidase-like catalytic activity, including carbon-based nanomaterials (carbon nanotubes, graphene oxide, and carbon nanodots) [15–17], metal oxides $\mathrm{\mathrm{(Fe}}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ) [18–20], macromolecule-based compounds (dendrimers, hydrogels, and polymers) [21–27], and other compounds [28–33]. Particularly, selenium-based mimicking systems have been extensively investigated due to their high glutathione peroxidase $(\mathrm{GPx})$ activity, which results from the catalytic reduction of hydroperoxides, with glutathione (GSH) serving as substrate. The Liu’s group reported a cyclodextrin-based enzyme model, in which $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -ditellurobis(2-deoxy- $\cdot\boldsymbol{\beta}$ -cyclodextrin) and 3-carboxy-4-nitrobenzenethiol were chosen as ${\mathrm{GPx}}$ mimic with desirable properties and preferential thiol substrate, respectively [23]. Zhang and co-workers introduced catalytic groups (selenium) into the core of dendrimers, successfully obtaining $\mathrm{GPx}$ mimics with high catalytic activity [21]. However, enzyme mimicking systems usually suffer from complicated preparation procedures and difficulties in the separation after the catalytic reactions. Therefore, novel enzyme mimics possessing high efficiency, excellent stability, easily recoverable and reusable properties eagerly need to be developed.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are composed of metal moieties and organic ligands, have received considerable attention in view of their unique features, such as the presence of specific functional groups, well-defined porosity and high surface area [34–42]. An enormous structural diversity can be rationally designed and synthesized by pre-selecting the metal precursors and/or organic ligands by postsynthetic modification (PSM), enabling the expansion of MOF applications in many fields, such as gas separation, heterogeneous catalysis, as well as sensors and biocatalysts [43–53]. Based on the tunable cages/channels of MOFs, a suitable environment around the catalytically active sites can be tailored, thus providing a regio, chemo and/or enantioselectivity that cannot be expected with natural enzymes [44]. In addition, the various structures allow MOFs to mimic certain features of the functional active sites of enzymes and thus perform as biomimetic catalysts [3, 43–46]. In this respect, some progresses have been recently achieved. The Ma’s group reported a biomimetic catalyst consisting of iron-based metal-metalloporphyrin frameworks named as MMPF-6 [43]. This material, which has been demonstrated to possess impressive peroxidase activity, consisted of zirconyl ions linked by iron (III) meso-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin chloride (Fe-TCPP-Cl) ligands. Larsen and co-workers encapsulated catalytically active metalloporphyrins within HKUST-1 (a prototypal MOF) to mimic the activity of a heme protein [45]. Compared with the applications of MOFs in other fields, the recent advances of MOF-based catalysts in biomimetic catalysis are still in their infancy, therefore requiring a further systematical and comprehensive exploration.
Herein, we developed a general and facile method to fabricate efficient catalysts acting as $\mathrm{GPx}$ mimics by grafting selenium-containing molecules (PhSeBr) to a $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ -based $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}{\-}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ framework. In this ${\mathrm{GPx}}$ mimic system, the selenium-containing molecules served as function donators, while the MOFs with high specific surface area and uniform porosity supplied more catalytic active centers creating a special catalytic environment, which resulted in a high catalytic activity. The high $\mathrm{GPx}$ -like catalytic activity of the seleniumcontaining MOF catalysts (UiO-66-Se), which also exhibited good reproducibility and cycling stability due to the stable UiO-66 framework, was demonstrated in a series of enzyme mimic promoted reactions.
## 2 Results and discussion
The UiO-66-Se mimic system endowed with good stability and high catalytic activity was obtained by PSM methods. The $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ framework (a typical MOF consisting of zirconium ions linked by 2-aminoterephthalic acid ligands) maintained good chemical and physical stability in various solvents and under elevated pressure conditions. Owing to their high $\operatorname{GPx}$ activity, selenium-based mimicking systems have been extensively investigated. Hence, it was envisaged that $\operatorname{GPx}$ mimic catalysts could be synthetized by combining the porous $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals as ideal matrices and a selenium-containing small molecule as catalytically active center. In this $\mathrm{GPx}$ mimic system, the new selenium-containing moiety (UiO-66-NH-Se- $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}}\mathrm{.}$ ) was produced by covalently linking PhSeBr to the pendant amine group of UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Scheme 1). On the basis of the above idea, the designed selenium-containing MOF catalysts could mimic the essential properties of the active site of natural $\mathrm{GPx}$ .
The as-prepared catalysts treated under mild conditions (RT, 3D) were chosen as model catalysts to be tested as enzyme mimics. The morphology, stability, porosity, and crystal structure of the UiO-66-Se catalyst were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) and powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) analyses. As it can be seen in Fig. 2, the single crystal particles of the UiO-66-Se samples with octahedral shapes had an average size ranging from 500 to $700\mathrm{nm}$ . Compared with $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig. S3 in the ESM), the morphology of UiO-66-Se exhibited no apparent change. Similarly, the shapes of the samples modified in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ were in good agreement with that of the parent UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . Based on the TGA analysis (Fig. S4 in the ESM), the weight of the parent UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UiO-66-Se was reduced to 68.6 and 71.4 at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively. The TGA curve results indicated a similar thermal stability of the two samples.
The XRD patterns of the as-prepared UiO-66-Se catalysts are shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM. Under different modified conditions, all the XRD patterns of UiO-66-Se exhibited well-defined peaks consistent with those of the simulated XRD patterns of UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ demonstrating that the MOF crystals were properly obtained after the PSM treatment. Similarly, the crystalline structure of UiO-66-Se clearly remained intact under the $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ treatment (Fig. S6 in the ESM). To verify the effect of the introduced seleniumcontaining molecules into the porous structure of the MOF scaffold, BET measurements were performed. From the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms (Fig. S7 in the ESM), it can be seen that the BET surface area of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ and UiO-66-Se was 847 and $641\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively. As expected, UiO-66-Se had a lower surface area than UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ which was presumably attributed to the steric effects of the introduced PhSeBr into the MOFs. Due to the high surface area of UiO-66-Se, the catalysts experimentally possessed many catalytic active centers, which contributed to their enhanced catalytic activity.
Following the introduction of PhSeBr into the porous MOFs, we investigated the GPx-like activity of the designed selenium-containing MOFs. To the best of our knowledge, the active ingredient of $\mathrm{GPx}$ with the selenium-containing active center, is a very important mammalian antioxidant enzyme that enables the superfluous harmful hydroperoxides (ROOH) to be effectively reduced to non-toxic hydroxyl compounds (ROH) using GSH as substrate [54, 55]. In present investigation, UiO-66-Se with three-dimensional nanoscale structure and functional group of PhSe-NH as a catalytic active center, which revealed that the catalytic mechanism of catalysts were analogous to these found the function of selenocysteine in the natural ${\mathrm{GPx}}$ active site [56]. According to this concept, the catalytic ability of the selenium-containing catalysts was tested via a slightly modified Hilvert method, in which a thiol substrate (3-carboxy-4-nitrobenzenethiol/ 4-nitrothiophenol (TNB/NBT), Scheme S1 in the ESM) was selected as GSH alternative. Specifically, the $\mathrm{GPx}-$ like catalytic reaction can be described as follows [57]
$$
2\mathrm{RSH}+\mathrm{ROOH}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{GPx}\mathrm{mimic}}\mathrm{RSSR}+\mathrm{ROH}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
The hydrophilic hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ and relatively hydrophobic cumenehydroperoxide (CUOOH) were chosen as substrates to measure the catalytic activity of UiO-66-Se; the catalytic activity plots are summarized in Fig. 3. The initial rate of the background (non-enzymatic) reaction in the TNB- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system was $2.53\upmu\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{min}$ (Table S2 in the ESM), and once different amounts of UiO-66-Se (10, 20, and $30\mathrm{mg}$ ) were added to the solution, the rate increased slightly to 2.86, 3.90, and $7.07\upmu\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{min},$ respectively. When $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was replaced by CUOOH (Table S3 in the ESM), the initial rate changed from 1.31 (non-enzymatic) to 1.97, 2.93, and $5.02\upmu\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{min}$ in the presence of UiO-66-Se (10, 20, and $30\mathrm{mg},$ ), respectively. The above results suggest that UiO-66-Se exhibited an apparent rate enhancement due to the presence of the MOF porous structure and more catalytic active centers. Evidently, after the porous UiO-66-Se $(30~\mathrm{mg})$ with exceptional catalytic activity was added, the rate in the TNB-CUOOH and TNB- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ systems was 3.83 and 2.79 times higher, respectively, leading to the maximum catalytic enhancement. To further test the catalytic activity of UiO-66-Se, we chose another thiol substrate (NBT) as glutathione alternative. By comparison, the UiO-66-Se exhibited much higher catalytic activity in the NBTCUOOH system (Table S4 in the ESM) than in the NBT- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system (Table S5 in the ESM). According to the two assay results, the catalytic activity for the reduction of the hydrophobic CUOOH was higher than that for the hydrophilic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ which is presumably due to the hydrophobic properties of UiO-66-Se. In the model system (Fig. S8 in the ESM), we also examined the catalytic ability of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ and UiO-66. In comparison to the above results, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UiO-66 showed almost no effects in the catalytic reactions.
Given the good catalytic activity observed with a volume ratio PBS and MeOH of $1/1.$ , the cycling stability of the UiO-66-Se system as $\mathrm{GPx}$ mimic was also conducted under the same conditions. As shown in Table 2, the UiO-66-Se catalysts were reused 3 times without significant loss of catalytic activity. In the reused ${\mathrm{GPx}}$ -mimics, upon addition of 10, 20, and $30\mathrm{mg}$ of UiO-66-Se in the third run, the initial rate became $108\%$ , $106\%$ , and $113\%$ , respectively, of that of the first run. This enhanced catalytic activity may mainly originate from the activated UiO-66-Se catalysts.
The SEM images of the reused UiO-66-Se showed a clear morphology similar to the fresh catalysts, indicating that no catalyst decomposition occurred during the reaction (Fig. S9 in the ESM). To verify the stability of the catalytic active center (Se–N bond), we conducted an $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis. After reuse in the catalytic reactions (Fig. S10 in the ESM), the peak relative to the selenium 3d orbitals showed no shift, indicating that selenium–nitrogen bond was very stable. According to the above results, the as-prepared UiO-66-Se can provide satisfactory cycling stability and high catalytic activity.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, a general and facile strategy has been developed for the synthesis of enzyme mimic catalysts through the covalent PSM approach. High efficiency $\mathrm{GPx}$ mimics were prepared by introducing small selenium-containing molecules into MOFs, wherein the introduced selenium-containing molecules play the role of function donators and the porous MOFs provide more catalytic active centers. In addition, the selenium-containing MOFs exhibited good catalytic activity over several cycles due to their excellent thermal and structural stability. Overall, these results show that the PSM approach can provide an excellent avenue for the production of high-efficiency $\operatorname{GPx}$ mimic MOFs.
